/* ==============================================================
   05 — Motion settings for joint moves
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     MCS500 only (R1)
   TEACHES   SetJointVel, SetJointAcc, SetJointVelLimit
   NEEDS     Robot activated, no tool mounted
   WATCH     The same move, four times, at four different speeds
   TIME      ~30 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   SETTINGS ARE NOT MOTIONS

   SetJointVel does not move anything. It changes a setting that
   every subsequent motion command obeys, until something changes
   it again.

   That is why tuning a program never means rewriting it. The
   targets stay; you change one number at the top.

   These settings ride in the motion queue alongside the moves,
   in order — so a SetJointVel placed halfway down the file takes
   effect exactly there, not immediately when the file is run.
   You can therefore have a fast approach and a slow final
   descent in the same program, just by putting the setting
   between them.

   THE THREE COMMANDS

       SetJointVel(p)        target joint velocity, % of top speed
       SetJointAcc(p)        joint acceleration, % of max
       SetJointVelLimit(p)   hard ceiling on joint velocity, %

   All percentages, from 0.001 to 100, because a joint's top
   speed is a property of that joint, not a number the user
   should have to look up. For the record, on the MCS500 those
   top speeds are 300 deg/s for joint 1, 500 deg/s for joint 2,
   700 mm/s for the shaft going up and down, and 5,000 deg/s for
   the shaft turning. SetJointVel(50) asks every joint for half
   of its own.

   SetJointVel vs SetJointVelLimit is the one people confuse:

     SetJointVel      the speed you are ASKING FOR on joint-space
                      moves: MoveJoints, MovePose, MoveJump. A
                      target.
     SetJointVelLimit a hard CEILING that applies to every Move*
                      command, including Cartesian ones and
                      velocity-mode ones.

   Use SetJointVel to tune a program. Use SetJointVelLimit to cap
   a whole program at once — for a first slow run, or to keep a
   fragile payload from being whipped around — without editing a
   single SetJointVel or SetCartLinVel line. It cannot be exceeded
   by anything downstream.

   One extra effect worth knowing: lowering SetJointVelLimit also
   SHORTENS the acceleration ramps, because a lower top speed is
   reached sooner. SetJointVelLimit(50) halves every acceleration
   phase. On short moves that can make a capped program feel
   brisker, not slower.

   /!\ SetJointVelLimit is not a safety limit.
   It is a convenience for the programmer, not a safety feature.

   ACCELERATION IS THE ONE PEOPLE FORGET

   On short moves, acceleration matters more than velocity. Over
   a short stroke the robot never reaches the speed you asked
   for; it spends the whole move accelerating and decelerating.
   If a short-stroke cycle feels slow, the answer is usually
   SetJointAcc, not SetJointVel — and short strokes are exactly
   what SCARAs spend their lives doing.

   Note that on the MCS500, SetJointAcc makes the robot come to a
   stop at that point in the queue, even with blending enabled.
   Set it before a sequence, not between two moves you want to
   flow together.

   DEFAULTS COME BACK

   These are reset to their defaults every time the robot is
   activated or reactivated: 25% velocity, 100% acceleration, 100%
   limit. A program that depends on a particular speed must set
   it, every run. Do not assume the value left over from the last
   session.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode, MoveJump
   settings — so this program starts from firmware defaults no
   matter what ran before it. Reactivating brings the motors
   back.

   This does NOT move the arm: the MCS500 has absolute encoders
   and never needs homing, which is why there is no Home() here.

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision, P-Stop 2 and calibration config
   all survive. Run 00_common/00_cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


// --- Baseline -------------------------------------------------
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(1)


// --- Slow: 10% ------------------------------------------------
// Demo speed. Legible, and comfortable to stand next to.
SetJointVel(10)
MoveJoints(60, 60, -40, 90)
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)

Delay(1)


// --- Moderate: 40% --------------------------------------------
// Identical target lines. Only the setting above them changed.
SetJointVel(40)
MoveJoints(60, 60, -40, 90)
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)

Delay(1)


// --- Full speed: 100% -----------------------------------------
// This is the number that ends the "is it fast enough?"
// conversation. Give people room before running it: at its top
// speed of 300 deg/s, joint 1 could cover this 60 degree swing
// in a fifth of a second, before acceleration is counted.
SetJointVel(100)
MoveJoints(60, 60, -40, 90)
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)

Delay(1)


// --- Full speed, low acceleration -----------------------------
// Velocity is still 100%, but the robot is now given a long,
// gentle ramp. It looks noticeably softer, and on a move this
// short it is actually slower overall — which is the lesson.
SetJointAcc(5)
MoveJoints(60, 60, -40, 90)
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)

Delay(1)


// --- Put the settings back ------------------------------------
// Leave the robot in a sane state for whoever runs the next
// example. Good practice in a shared demo cell.
SetJointAcc(100)
SetJointVel(15)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. Run the whole file without commentary and watch the four
      passes. The point makes itself.

   2. Add SetJointVelLimit(20) at the very top and run again.
      Every pass is now slow, including the 100% one. That is
      the difference between a target and a ceiling. (Put
      SetJointVelLimit(100) back at the end, or the next example
      inherits it until the robot is reactivated.)

   NEXT   06_linear_motion_settings.mxprog — the same idea for
          Cartesian moves, where the units are real ones.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
